 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that uses regular expressions and string manipulation to switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Define a list of common English adjectives for identification
  const adjectives = /([A-Z][a-z]+( [A-Z][a-z]+)*\b)( [A-Z][a-z]+( [A-Z][a-z]+)*\b)/g;

  let match;
  let newSentence = sentence;

  while ((match = adjectives.exec(sentence)) !== null) {
    const firstAdj = match[1];
    const secondAdjAndNoun = match[2];

    // Split the matched words into separate variables
    const words = sentence.split(' ');
    const index = words.indexOf(firstAdj);
    const nextIndex = words.findIndex((word, i) => i > index && word === secondAdjAndNoun);

    // Swap the positions of the two adjectives
    [words[index], words[nextIndex]] = [words[nextIndex], words[index]];

    // Reconstruct the sentence from the modified array
    newSentence = words.join(' ') + ' ';
  }

  return newSentence.trim();
}
// Test the function with your example sentence
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Beautiful green trees are in the park")); // "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```
This function uses a regular expression to identify pairs of adjacent adjectives in a sentence. It then swaps their positions and constructs a new sentence by joining the words with spaces. This approach assumes that there are only two adjectives between any given pair of articles or other determiners, but it should work for most simple sentences. If your input sentences contain more complex structures, you may need to modify the function accordingly.

